<h3 id="qlim7" class="tex-subsection"><span data-tag="328952">8.2.8</span> Diagram Categories and Homotopy (Co)limits</h3>

<p>
Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category and $\calC $ a small category. We let $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ denote the category of all functors from $\calC $ to $\mathbf{A}$. In this section, we will see that $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ again admits the structure of a combinatorial model category: in fact, it admits two such structures. Moreover, by considering the functoriality of this construction in the category $\calC $, we will obtain the theory of <em>homotopy limits</em> and <em>homotopy colimits</em>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="AECA43">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/AECA43">Definition <span data-tag="AECA43">8.2.8.1</span>.</a>cofibration!projectivecofibration!injectivefibration!injectivefibration!projectiveinjective!cofibrationinjective!fibrationprojective!cofibrationprojective!fibration Let $\calC $ be a small category, and $\mathbf{A}$ a model category. A natural transformation $\alpha : F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is a: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li><p>
<em>injective cofibration</em> if the induced map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ is a cofibration in $\mathbf{A}$, for each $C \in \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>projective fibration</em> if the induced map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ is a fibration in $\mathbf{A}$, for each $C \in \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>weak equivalence</em> if the induced map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathbf{A}$, for each $C \in \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>injective fibration</em> if it has the right lifting property with respect to every morphism $\beta $ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ which is simultaneously a weak equivalence and a injective cofibration. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>projective cofibration</em> if it has the left lifting property with respect to every morphism $\beta $ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ which is simultaneously a weak equivalence and a projective fibration. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="B160E2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/B160E2">Proposition <span data-tag="B160E2">8.2.8.2</span>.</a>model category!projectivemodel category!injective Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category and $\calC $ be a small category. Then there exist two combinatorial model structures on $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li><p>
The <em>projective model structure</em>, determined by the strong cofibrations, weak equivalences, and projective fibrations. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The <em>injective model structure</em>, determined by the weak cofibrations, weak equivalences, and injective fibrations. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
The following is the key step in the proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0533E9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0533E9">Lemma <span data-tag="0533E9">8.2.8.3</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a presentable category, and $\calC $ a small category. Let $S_0$ be a (small) set of morphisms of $\mathbf{A}$, and let $\overline{S}_0$ be the weakly saturated class of morphisms generated by $S_0$. Let $\widetilde{S}$ be the collection of all morphisms $F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ with the following property: for every $C \in \calC $, the map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ belongs to $\overline{S}_0$. Then there exists a (small) set of morphisms $S$ of $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ which generates $\widetilde{S}$ as a weakly saturated class of morphisms. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
We will prove a generalization of Lemma <a href="/tag/0533E9" data-tag="0533E9">8.2.8.3</a> in §<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a> (Lemma <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>). 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/B160E2" data-tag="B160E2">8.2.8.2</a>.</strong>
       We first treat the case of the projective model structure. For each For each object $C \in \calC $ and each $A \in \mathbf{A}$, we define 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ \calF ^ C_ A: \calC \rightarrow \mathbf{A} \]
</div>
    <p>
       by the formulaFcalCA@$\calF ^{C}_{A}$ 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ \calF ^ C_ A(C') = \coprod _{ \alpha \in \bHom _{\calC }(C,C') } A. \]
</div>
    <p>
       We note that if $i: A \rightarrow A'$ is a (trivial) cofibration in $\mathbf{A}$, then the induced map $\calF ^ C_{A} \rightarrow \calF ^ C_{A'}$ is a strong (trivial) cofibration in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$. 
    <p>
      Let $I_0$ be a set of generating cofibrations $i: A \rightarrow B$ for $\mathbf{A}$, and let $I$ be the set of all induced maps $\calF ^{C}_{A} \rightarrow \calF ^{C}_{B}$ (where $C$ ranges over $\calC $. Let $J_0$ be a set of generating trivial cofibrations for $\mathbf{A}$, and define $J$ likewise. It follows immediately from the definitions that a morphism in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is a projective fibration if and only if it has the right lifting property with respect to every morphism in $J$, and a weak trivial fibration if and only if it has the right lifting property with respect to every morphism in $I$. Let $\overline{I}$ and $\overline{J}$ be the weakly saturated classes of morphisms of $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ generated by $I$ and $J$, respectively. Using the small object argument, we deduce: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
Every morphism $f: X \rightarrow Z$ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ admits a factorization 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  X \stackrel{f'}{\rightarrow } Y \stackrel{f''}{\rightarrow } Z \]
</div>
<p>
 where $f' \in \overline{I}$ and $f''$ is a weak trivial fibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
Every morphism $f: X \rightarrow Z$ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ admits a factorization 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  X \stackrel{f'}{\rightarrow } Y \stackrel{f''}{\rightarrow } Z \]
</div>
<p>
 where $f' \in \overline{J}$ and $f''$ is a projective fibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iii)$"><p>
The class $\overline{I}$ coincides with the class of projective cofibrations in $\mathbf{A}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
       Furthermore, since the class of trivial projective cofibrations in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is weakly saturated and contains $J$, it contains $\overline{J}$. This proves that $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ satisfies the factorization axioms. The only other nontrivial point to check is that $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ satisfies the lifting axioms. Consider a diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { A \ar [d]^{i} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{p} \\ C \ar [r] \ar @{-->}[ur] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
    <p>
       in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$, where $i$ is a projective cofibration and $p$ is a projective fibration. We wish to show that there exists a dotted arrow as indicated, provided that either $i$ or $p$ is a weak equivalence. If $p$ is a weak equivalence then this follows immediately from the definition of a injective fibration. Suppose instead that $i$ is a trivial projective cofibration. We wish to show that $i$ has the left lifting property with respect to every projective fibration. It will suffice to show every trivial injective fibration belongs to $\overline{J}$ (this will also show that $J$ is a set of generating trivial cofibrations for $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$, which will show that the projective model structure on $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is combinatorial). Suppose then that $i$ is a trivial weak coibration, and choose a factorization 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  A \stackrel{i'}{\rightarrow } B \stackrel{i''}{\rightarrow } C \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $i' \in \overline{J}$ and $i''$ is a projective fibration. Then $i'$ is a weak equivalence, so that $i''$ is a weak equivalence by the two-out-of-three property. Consider the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { A \ar [d]^{i} \ar [r]^{i'} &amp;  B \ar [d]^{i''} \\ C \ar [r]^{=} \ar @{-->}[ur] &amp;  C} \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $i$ is a cofibration, there exists a dotted arrow as indicated. This proves that $i$ is a retract of $i'$, and therefore belongs to $\overline{J}$ as desired. 
    <p>
      We now prove the existence of the injective model structure on $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$. Here it is difficult to proceed directly, so we will instead apply Proposition <a href="/tag/C44CD9" data-tag="C44CD9">8.2.6.8</a>. It will suffice to check each of the hypotheses in turn: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The collection of injective cofibrations in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is generated (as a weakly saturated class) by some small set of morphisms. This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The collection of trivial injective cofibrations in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is weakly saturated: this follows immediately from the fact that the class of injective cofibrations in $\mathbf{A}$ is weakly saturated. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
The collection of weak equivalences in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is an accessible subcategory of $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})^{[1]}$: this follows from the proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/24A026" data-tag="24A026">5.4.4.3</a>, since the collection of weak equivalences in $\mathbf{A}$ form an accessible subcategory of $\mathbf{A}^{[1]}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(4)$"><p>
The collection of weak equivalences in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ satisfy the two-out-of-three property: this follows immediately from the fact that the weak equivalences in $\mathbf{A}$ satisfy the two-out-of-three property. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(5)$"><p>
Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism in $\mathbf{A}$ which has the right lifting property with respect to every injective cofibration. In particular, $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to each of the morphisms in the class $I$ defined above, so that $f$ is a trivial projective fibration, and in particular a weak equivalence. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="B2AACC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/B2AACC">Remark <span data-tag="B2AACC">8.2.8.4</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a href="/tag/B160E2" data-tag="B160E2">8.2.8.2</a>, if $\mathbf{A}$ is assumed to be right or left proper, then $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is likewise right or left proper (with respect to either the projective or the injective model structures). </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="486231">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/486231">Remark <span data-tag="486231">8.2.8.5</span>.</a> It follows from the proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a> that the class of projective cofibrations is generated (as a weakly saturated class of morphisms) by the maps $j: \calF ^{C}_{A} \rightarrow \calF ^{C}_{A'}$, where $C \in \calC $ and $A \rightarrow A'$ is a cofibration in $\mathbf{A}$. We observe that $j$ is a injective cofibration. It follows that every projective cofibration is a injective cofibration; dually, every injective fibration is a projective fibration. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="DE1B77">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/DE1B77">Remark <span data-tag="DE1B77">8.2.8.6</span>.</a> The construction of Proposition <a href="/tag/B160E2" data-tag="B160E2">8.2.8.2</a> is functorial in the following sense: given a Quillen adjunction of combinatorial model categories $\xymatrix {\mathbf{A} \ar @< .4ex>[r]^{F} &amp;  \mathbf{B} \ar @< .4ex>[l]^{G}}$ and a small category $\calC $, composition with $F$ and $G$ determines a Quillen adjunction </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix {\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A}) \ar @< .4ex>[r]^{ F^{\calC }} &amp;  \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{B}) \ar @< .4ex>[l]^{G^{\calC }}} \]
</div>
  <p> (with respect to either the injective or the projective model structures). Moreover, if $(F,G)$ is a Quillen equivalence, then so is $( F^{\calC }, G^{\calC } )$. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
Because the projective and injective model structures on $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ have the same weak equivalences, the identity functor $\id _{\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})}$ is a Quillen equivalence between them. However, it is important to keep distinguish these two model structures, because they have different variance properties as we now explain. 
</p>
<p>
Let $f: \calC \rightarrow \calC '$ be a functor between small categories. Then composition with $f$ yields a pullback functor $f^{\ast }: \Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A}) \rightarrow \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$. Since $\mathbf{A}$ admits small limits and colimits, $f^{\ast }$ has a right adjoint which we shall denote by $f_{\ast }$ and a left adjoint which we shall denote by $f_{!}$. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="057F45">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/057F45">Proposition <span data-tag="057F45">8.2.8.7</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category, and let $f: \calC \rightarrow \calC '$ be a functor between small categories. Then: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The pair $( f_{!}, f^{\ast } )$ determines a Quillen adjunction between the <em>projective</em> model structures on $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ and $\Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A})$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The pair $( f^{\ast }, f_{\ast } )$ determines a Quillen adjunction between the <em>injective</em> model structures on $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ and $\Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A})$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows immediately from the simple observation that $f^{\ast }$ preserves weak equivalences, projective fibrations, and weak cofibrations. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
We now review the theory of homotopy limits and colimits in a combinatorial model category $\mathbf{A}$. For simplicity, we will discuss homotopy limits and leave the analogous theory of homotopy colimits to the reader. Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category, and let $f: \calC \rightarrow \calC '$ be functor betweeen (small) categories. We wish to consider the right-derived functor $Rf_{\ast }$ of the right Kan extension $f_{\ast }: \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A}) \rightarrow \Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A})$. This derived functor is called the <em>homotopy right Kan extension</em> functor. The usual way of defining it involves choosing a “fibrant replacement functor” $Q: \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A}) \rightarrow \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$, and setting $Rf_{\ast } = f_{\ast } \circ Q$. The assumption that $\mathbf{A}$ is combinatorial guarantees that such a fibrant replacement functor exists. However, for our purposes it is more convenient to address the indeterminacy in the definition of $Rf_{\ast }$ in another way. 
</p>
<p>
Let $F \in \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$, $G \in \Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A})$, and let $\eta : G \rightarrow f_{\ast } F$ be a map in $\Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A})$. We will say that $\eta $ <em>exhibits $G$ as the homotopy right Kan extension of $F$</em> if, for some weak equivalence $F \rightarrow F'$ where $F'$ is injectively fibrant in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$, the composite map $G \rightarrow f_{\ast } F \rightarrow f_{\ast } F'$ is a weak equivalence in $\Fun (\calC ', \mathbf{A})$. Since $f_{\ast }$ preserves weak equivalences between injectively fibrant objects, this condition is independent of the choice of $F'$.Kan extension!homotopy 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">8.2.8.8</span>.</a>Given an object $F \in \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$, it is not necessarily the case that there exists a map $\eta : G \rightarrow f_{\ast } F$ which exhibits $G$ as a homotopy right Kan extension of $F$. However, such a map can always be found after replacing $F$ by a weakly equivalent object; for example, if $F$ is injectively fibrant, we may take $G = f_{\ast } F$ and $\eta $ to be the identity map. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
Let $[0]$ denote the final object of $\Cat $: that is, the category with one object and only the identity morphism. For <em>any</em> category $\calC $, there is a unique functor $f: \calC \rightarrow [0]$. If $\mathbf{A}$ is a combinatorial model category, $F: \calC \rightarrow \mathbf{A}$ a functor, and $A \in \mathbf{A}\simeq \Fun ( [0], \mathbf{A})$ is an object, then we will say that a natural transformation $\alpha : f^{\ast } A \rightarrow F$ <em>exhibits $A$ as a homotopy limit of $F$</em> if it exhibits $A$ as a homotopy right Kan extension of $F$. Note that we can identify $\alpha $ with a map $A \rightarrow \lim _{C \in \calC } F(C)$ in the model category $\mathbf{A}$. 
</p>
<p>
The theory of homotopy right Kan extensions in general can be reduced to the theory of homotopy limits, in view of the following result: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="B3A655">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/B3A655">Proposition <span data-tag="B3A655">8.2.8.9</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category, let $f: \calC \rightarrow \calD $ be a functor between small categories, and let $F: \calC \rightarrow \mathbf{A}$ and $G: \calD \rightarrow \mathbf{A}$ be diagrams. A natural transformation $\alpha : f^{\ast } G \rightarrow F$ exhibits $G$ as a homotopy right Kan extension of $F$ if and only if, for each object $D \in \calD $, $\alpha $ exhibits $G(D)$ as a homotopy limit of the composite diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  F_{D/}: \calC \times _{ \calD } \calD _{D/} \rightarrow \calC \stackrel{F}{\rightarrow } \mathbf{A}.  \]
</div>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
To prove Proposition <a href="/tag/B3A655" data-tag="B3A655">8.2.8.9</a>, we can immediately reduce to the case where $F$ is a injectively fibrant diagram. In this case, $\alpha $ exhibits $G$ as a homotopy right Kan extension of $F$ if and only if it induces a weak homotopy equivalence $G(D) \rightarrow \lim F_{D/}$, for each $D \in \calD $. It will therefore suffice to prove the following result (in the case $\calC ' = \calC \times _{\calD } \calD _{D/}$): 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="43C621">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/43C621">Lemma <span data-tag="43C621">8.2.8.10</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category and $g: \calC ' \rightarrow \calC $ a functor which exhibits $\calC '$ as cofibered in sets over $\calC $. Then the pullback functor $g^{\ast }: \Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A}) \rightarrow \Fun ( \calC ', \mathbf{A})$ preserves injective fibrations. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It will suffice to show that the left adjoint $g_{!}$ preserves weak trivial cofibrations. Let $\alpha : F \rightarrow F'$ be a map in $\Fun ( \calC ', \mathbf{A})$. We observe that for each object $C \in \calC $, the map $(q_{!} \alpha )(C): (q_{!} F)(C) \rightarrow (q_{!} F')(C)$ can be identified with the coproduct of the maps $\{  \alpha (C'): F(C') \rightarrow F'(C') \} _{ C' \in g^{-1} \{ C\}  }$. If $\alpha $ is a weak trivial cofibration, then each of these maps is a trivial cofibration in $\mathbf{A}$, so that $q_{!} \alpha $ is again a weak trivial cofibration as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">8.2.8.11</span>.</a>In the preceding discussion, we considered injective model structures, $Rf_{\ast }$, and homotopy limits. An entirely dual discussion may be carried out with projective model structures and $Lf_{!}$; one obtains a notion of <em>homotopy colimit</em> which is the dual of the notion of homotopy limit. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Example <span data-tag="">8.2.8.12</span>.</a>Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a combinatorial model category, and consider a diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  X' \stackrel{f}{\leftarrow } X \stackrel{g}{\rightarrow } X''. \]
</div>
  <p> This diagram is projectively cofibrant if and only if the object $X$ is cofibrant, and the maps $f$ and $g$ are both cofibrations. Consequently, the definition of homotopy colimits given above recovers, as a special case, the theory of homotopy pushouts presented in §<a href="/tag/1D0C11" data-tag="1D0C11">8.2.4</a>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
